---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import PartnerOffers from '../../components/PartnerOffers.astro';
import { articleSchema, faqSchema } from '../../lib/structured-data';

const title = 'Beginner IT homelab equipment: what you actually need, and what can wait';
const description = 'What a beginner IT homelab really needs, based on a six-VM Active Directory and Linux lab that runs on one laptop. What to buy first, what to add later, and what to skip.';
const structuredData = [
  articleSchema({
    headline: title,
    description,
    url: 'https://stackgeist.dev/labs/homelab-equipment-for-beginners/',
    datePublished: '2026-11-05',
    dateModified: '2026-11-05',
  }),
  faqSchema({ questions: [
    { question: 'Do I need a server for an IT homelab?', answer: 'No. The six-VM lab behind this page runs on one laptop with VMware Workstation. A server or mini PC only helps when you want something running all the time, or your PC cannot take more RAM.' },
    { question: 'What should I buy first for a homelab?', answer: 'Usually nothing. Start with the computer you own. When it runs out of memory, add RAM first. Then add storage space, and an external drive for backups.' },
    { question: 'Do I need a network switch for a homelab?', answer: 'Not for a lab that runs as virtual machines on one computer, because the hypervisor provides the network. You need a switch only when you add physical machines that have to talk to each other over cables.' },
    { question: 'How much RAM does a beginner homelab need?', answer: 'Plan on 32 GB of host RAM for a domain controller, file server, and Windows 11 client. The author’s six-VM lab assigns 36 GB to its VMs on a 64 GB host.' },
  ] }),
];

const tiers = [
  {
    id: 'now',
    kicker: 'Need now',
    heading: 'What every beginner lab needs',
    items: [
      { name: 'The computer you already own', why: 'A modern PC with hardware virtualization can run your first VMs. Mine is a laptop.', check: 'Turn on AMD-V (SVM) or Intel VT-x in the UEFI settings, and free up space before buying anything.' },
      { name: 'Virtualization software', why: 'This lab runs on VMware Workstation. It provides the VMs and the virtual network between them, so no physical network gear is needed.', check: 'Check the current licensing terms on the vendor’s site before installing.' },
      { name: 'An external drive for backups', why: 'Backups and recovery drills are real help desk skills. I own a 4 TB WD My Passport portable hard drive.', check: 'One external drive is the first copy, not a complete backup plan.' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'later',
    kicker: 'Add when you hit a limit',
    heading: 'Upgrades that actually help',
    items: [
      { name: 'More RAM', why: 'RAM decides how many VMs run at once. Going from 16 to 32 GB is the biggest single improvement.', check: 'Check your model’s maximum and whether the RAM is soldered before buying.' },
      { name: 'More SSD space', why: 'My six VMs use about 202 GB, and virtual disks keep growing.', check: 'Keep at least 250 GB free for a lab this size, plus room for snapshots.' },
      { name: 'A USB-to-SATA adapter', why: 'Lets you practice pulling files off old laptop drives, a common support task.', check: 'Match SATA or NVMe to the drive you want to read.' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'wait',
    kicker: 'Only when you need it',
    heading: 'Gear that can wait',
    items: [
      { name: 'A network switch', why: 'Only needed once you add physical machines. My lab has none, because everything runs on VMware’s virtual network.', check: 'An unmanaged gigabit switch is enough until you need VLANs.' },
      { name: 'A mini PC', why: 'Useful as an always-on host, or when your PC can’t take more RAM.', check: 'Look for low idle power, 8 cores, and support for 64 GB.' },
      { name: 'A UPS', why: 'Worth it once the lab runs services or stores data you care about.', check: 'Test one automatic shutdown before you rely on it.' },
    ],
  },
];
---
<ContentLayout title="Beginner IT homelab equipment: what you need first | StackGeist" description={description} active="labs" structuredData={structuredData}>
  <article class="article-page">
    <p class="breadcrumbs"><a href="/labs/">Labs</a> / Homelab equipment for beginners</p>
    <p class="page-kicker">Buying guide · based on a working lab</p>
    <h1 class="page-title">Beginner IT homelab equipment: what you actually need.</h1>
    <p class="page-lead">My homelab runs a domain controller, a file server, a Windows 11 client, and three Linux machines. It has no server, no rack, and no network switch. It runs on one laptop. Here is what that taught me about what to buy, and what to leave on the shelf.</p>

    <div class="article-summary"><strong>The short version</strong><p>Start with the computer you own and free virtualization software. Add an external drive for backups early, because backup and recovery are skills you should practice. When you run out of room, add RAM first, then SSD space. A switch, a mini PC, and a UPS only matter once you add physical machines or always-on services.</p></div>

    <div class="article-body">
      <section>
        <p class="page-kicker">The lab behind this guide</p>
        <h2>What it runs on</h2>
        <div class="article-cards">
          <div><strong>Host</strong><p>A Lenovo laptop with a Ryzen 7 7435HS (8 cores), 64 GB of RAM, and a 4 TB NVMe SSD.</p></div>
          <div><strong>VMs</strong><p>Six, set to 36 GB of RAM and 16 virtual CPUs in total, using about 202 GB of disk.</p></div>
          <div><strong>Network</strong><p>VMware’s built-in NAT network. No physical switch or router of its own.</p></div>
        </div>
        <p>The full breakdown, VM by VM, is in <a href="/labs/vmware-homelab-hardware/">what hardware you need for a VMware homelab</a>.</p>
      </section>

      {tiers.map((tier) => (
        <section id={tier.id}>
          <p class="page-kicker">{tier.kicker}</p>
          <h2>{tier.heading}</h2>
          <div class="article-cards">
            {tier.items.map((item) => (
              <div><strong>{item.name}</strong><p>{item.why}</p><p class="tier-check"><span>Check:</span> {item.check}</p></div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </section>
      ))}

      <section>
        <p class="page-kicker">Skip for now</p>
        <h2>What beginners usually don’t need</h2>
        <ul class="article-list">
          <li><strong>A rack or a used enterprise server.</strong> They’re loud, use a lot of power, and teach the same skills a laptop can at this stage.</li>
          <li><strong>A managed switch.</strong> Only useful once you have physical machines and want to practice VLANs.</li>
          <li><strong>A NAS appliance.</strong> A file server VM and an external drive teach shares, permissions, and backups first.</li>
          <li><strong>10 gigabit networking.</strong> A starter lab won’t use it.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section>
        <p class="page-kicker">Buying order</p>
        <h2>If you buy anything, buy it in this order</h2>
        <ol class="article-steps">
          <li><strong>Nothing yet</strong><span>Build a domain controller and one client on the computer you own.</span></li>
          <li><strong>External drive</strong><span>Back up the lab and practice restoring a deleted file.</span></li>
          <li><strong>RAM</strong><span>When you can’t run the VMs you need at the same time.</span></li>
          <li><strong>SSD space</strong><span>When virtual disks and snapshots fill the drive.</span></li>
          <li><strong>Switch, mini PC, UPS</strong><span>Only when you add physical machines or always-on services.</span></li>
        </ol>
        <p><a href="/downloads/help-desk-homelab-starter-checklist.pdf" download>Download the printable homelab starter checklist (PDF)</a>, with skill drills to run once the lab is up.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <p class="page-kicker">How this guide was made</p>
        <p>The lab details come from my own setup and its documentation, read on 5 November 2026. I own the WD My Passport drive mentioned above. The other products are categories, not specific recommendations, and I haven’t benchmarked any of them.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <p class="page-kicker">Related</p>
        <h2>Next steps</h2>
        <div class="article-cards">
          <div><strong><a href="/labs/active-directory-home-lab/">Active Directory home lab</a></strong><p>Build the domain controller first, and see what an audit found.</p></div>
          <div><strong><a href="/labs/help-desk-practice-without-experience/">Practice help desk work</a></strong><p>What to do with the lab once it’s running.</p></div>
          <div><strong><a href="/guides/back-up-windows-pc-before-replacing-drive/">Back up before replacing a drive</a></strong><p>The same backup habits, applied to your own PC.</p></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <PartnerOffers ids={['homelabRam', 'nvmeSsd', 'externalBackupDrive', 'miniPc']} placement="lab-homelab-equipment-for-beginners" />

      <section>
        <p class="page-kicker">Sources</p>
        <ol class="article-list">
          <li>My lab documentation and VM configuration files, 5 November 2026.</li>
          <li><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/get-started/hardware-requirements">Microsoft Learn — Hardware requirements for Windows Server</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/windows-11-specifications">Microsoft — Windows 11 specifications and system requirements</a></li>
        </ol>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
  <style>
    .tier-check { margin-top:10px !important; padding-top:10px; border-top:1px solid var(--term-border); color:var(--quiet) !important; font-size:.875rem !important; }
    .tier-check span { color:var(--phosphor); font-family:var(--font-mono); font-size:.75rem; text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:.04em; }
  </style>
</ContentLayout>
